Smart Weather Station with Cloud Integration


Akshat Venugopal
12/2/2025
5


Abstract

The purpose of my experiment was to serve people who can't afford high-level air quality monitors by providing a cheap and functional alternative with cloud connectivity. How accurate is a homemade ESP32 air quality station compared to online weather/air-quality services, and how effectively can it transmit data to the cloud for remote monitoring? If weather conditions change, then air pollution levels will also change, because weather affects how dust particles and gases spread or settle in the air. The procedure involves placing the device in a location and allowing it to monitor the air around it while continuously sending data to AWS IoT Core for storage, analysis, and remote access. The system enables real-time monitoring from anywhere through cloud-based dashboards and historical data analysis. The results of this air quality monitoring showed varying pollution levels influenced by environmental factors and global conditions. The integration with AWS IoT allows for long-term data collection, trend analysis, and the ability to share information with multiple users simultaneously, making environmental data more accessible to communities that need it most.


Introduction

Air quality is an important part of the environment because it affects human health, comfort, and daily activities. Air pollution is mainly caused by smoke, dust, vehicle exhaust, chemicals, and natural particles in the air. Scientists use sensors to measure different parts of air quality, such as PM2.5 dust particles (tiny particles harmful to breathe), gases, humidity, and temperature. Weather conditions also influence pollution levels. For example, humidity can make particles heavier and cause them to fall, while temperature changes can trap pollution close to the ground.

Affordable air monitors help communities understand their environment without needing expensive equipment. However, traditional low-cost monitors have limitations: they only display data locally, cannot be accessed remotely, and don't store historical information for analysis. Modern cloud computing platforms like Amazon Web Services (AWS) Internet of Things (IoT) solve these problems by allowing devices to send data to the cloud, where it can be stored indefinitely, analyzed for patterns, and accessed from anywhere in the world through a web browser or mobile app.

How can I design an affordable air monitor that can efficiently report air quality data both locally and to the cloud for remote access and long-term storage? This question guides the engineering design process by identifying what current air monitors are missing—they are often expensive, inaccessible, too complex, or limited to local display only. This project aims to create a solution that is low-cost, effective, and connected to modern cloud infrastructure.

The purpose of this project is to design and build an affordable air monitoring device that can measure and report important environmental conditions such as temperature, humidity, gas levels, PM2.5 air pollution, and brightness, while transmitting this data to AWS IoT Core for cloud-based storage and analysis. Many people do not have access to expensive commercial air monitors, even though air quality affects health, comfort, and safety every day. By creating a low-cost device using easily available sensors and free cloud services, this project aims to give students, families, and communities a simple way to understand the quality of the air around them and track changes over time.

The integration with AWS IoT provides several advantages: it enables remote monitoring from any location with internet access, allows multiple users to view the same data simultaneously, provides automatic data backup and storage, enables historical trend analysis to identify patterns over days or weeks, and can trigger alerts when air quality reaches dangerous levels. Learning this information is valuable because it helps people make better decisions, such as when to go outside, when to open windows, or when the environment might be unsafe for those with allergies or breathing problems. This project shows how technology and engineering, combined with cloud computing, can be used to solve real-world problems in a cost-effective and meaningful way.

If I design and build an air monitoring system using low-cost sensors connected to AWS IoT Core, then it will be able to accurately measure and report air quality, temperature, humidity, and brightness in real time, provide cloud-based data storage for historical analysis, enable remote monitoring from any device, and generate AI-powered insights about environmental conditions and trends.


Methods

The assembly of the Affordable Air Monitor begins with gathering the essential electronic components, starting with the ESP32 microcontroller, which functions as the central processing unit of the system and provides built-in Wi-Fi connectivity for cloud communication. Following this, the MQ-series gas sensor is prepared to enable detection of harmful gases in the environment. Individual sensors for measuring temperature, humidity, and brightness are then collected. A breadboard and jumper wires are arranged to create the foundational circuit layout.

After all components are ready, each sensor is connected to the ESP32 in sequence, ensuring proper wiring and stable electrical connections. A suitable power source is added to activate the circuit. Once the hardware assembly is complete, the ESP32 is programmed with firmware that reads sensor data at regular intervals and formats it for transmission to the cloud.

The cloud integration process involves several steps. First, an AWS account is created to access AWS IoT Core services. Within the AWS IoT console, a new "Thing" is registered to represent the physical air monitor device. Security certificates are generated and downloaded to ensure encrypted communication between the device and the cloud. These certificates are then uploaded to the ESP32's memory. The device is configured with Wi-Fi credentials to enable internet connectivity.

The ESP32 is programmed to connect to the local Wi-Fi network, authenticate with AWS IoT Core using the security certificates, and publish sensor readings to specific MQTT topics at defined intervals (typically every 30-60 seconds). AWS IoT Core receives these messages and can route them to various services: data is stored in a database for historical analysis, real-time dashboards display current readings, and rules can trigger notifications when readings exceed safe thresholds.

The data pipeline works as follows: sensors measure environmental conditions → ESP32 processes the readings → data is formatted as JSON messages → messages are transmitted via Wi-Fi to AWS IoT Core → AWS stores and processes the data → users access information through web dashboards or mobile applications from anywhere in the world.

This architecture enables continuous monitoring without requiring a computer to be constantly connected to the device, and allows multiple users to access the same data simultaneously from different locations.


