Conclusion

The results of this experiment demonstrate that an affordable air quality monitoring system can be successfully built using readily available components and integrated with cloud infrastructure for enhanced functionality. The ESP32-based device successfully measured environmental parameters and transmitted data to AWS IoT Core with high reliability.

Comparing the results to the hypothesis, the data supported the prediction that a low-cost system could accurately measure air quality and provide real-time reporting when connected to cloud services. The integration with AWS IoT exceeded initial expectations by enabling features that would be impossible with local-only monitoring: historical data analysis, remote access from multiple devices, automated data backup, and the potential for AI-powered insights.

The scientific reasoning behind the results relates to both the sensor technology and cloud architecture. The sensors provided accurate measurements within their specified ranges, while the MQTT protocol and AWS IoT Core ensured reliable data transmission even with intermittent internet connectivity. The ESP32's built-in Wi-Fi capabilities proved essential for bridging the gap between physical sensors and cloud infrastructure.

This project relates to real-world situations in multiple ways. Communities in areas with poor air quality could deploy multiple units to create a neighborhood monitoring network, with all devices reporting to a shared AWS dashboard. Schools could use this system for science education while also monitoring their campus environment. Individuals with respiratory conditions could monitor their home air quality remotely and receive alerts on their phones when conditions deteriorate. The low cost makes it accessible to developing regions where commercial monitors are prohibitively expensive, but where air quality monitoring is critically needed.

Based on the results, suggestions include: deploying multiple units to compare readings across different locations, implementing automated alerts through AWS SNS when pollution levels exceed health thresholds, using AWS Lambda functions to analyze trends and predict future air quality, and creating mobile applications that provide user-friendly access to the data.

If this project were to be repeated, improvements would include: adding a battery backup to maintain monitoring during power outages, implementing local data caching to handle temporary internet connectivity loss, using a weatherproof enclosure for outdoor installation, calibrating sensors against professional reference instruments to ensure accuracy, and adding GPS capabilities for mobile monitoring applications.

Potential errors that could have affected results include: sensor drift over time requiring recalibration, Wi-Fi connectivity issues affecting data transmission consistency, placement of sensors affecting readings (e.g., too close to heat sources), and initial configuration challenges with AWS security certificates.

Future research directions include: implementing machine learning models in AWS SageMaker to predict air quality trends, integrating with weather forecast APIs to correlate predictions with actual measurements, creating a public API that allows third-party applications to access the data, developing a complete fleet management system for deploying hundreds of monitors across a city, and exploring edge computing capabilities to perform preliminary data analysis on the ESP32 before sending to AWS (reducing data transmission costs and enabling faster local alerts).

The next step is to deploy a pilot network of 5-10 devices across different locations, all reporting to the same AWS IoT infrastructure, to validate the system's scalability and to begin collecting meaningful comparative data across different environments.


